  {
   "id": 3,
   "ref": "124–128",
   "en": "and Dido and the Trojan leader will find their way to the very same cave. I will be there, and if I can be sure of your goodwill, I will join them in a lasting marriage, and make her his own. This will be their wedding.' Venus, who had no wish to oppose her as she asked, agreed, and smiled at the trick that she had seen through.",
   "en_marked": "and [Dido|2] [and|4] the [Trojan|5] [leader|3] [will find their way to|7] the [very same|6] [cave|1]. [I will be there|8], [and|9] [if|11] [I|12] can be [sure|13] of [your|10] [goodwill|14], [I will join|16] {them|Added for clarity: the Latin leaves the object of iungam unstated, so the translator supplies Dido and Aeneas.} in a [lasting|17] [marriage|15], [and|18] [make|19] {her his|Added for clarity: propriam (his own) is feminine and refers to Dido, so the translator makes the ownership explicit.} [own|18]. [This|20] [will be|22] their [wedding|21].' [Venus|30], who had [no|23] {wish|Not in the Latin. Added to smooth the English: non adversata simply means not opposing.} [to oppose|24] [her as she asked|25], [agreed|26], [and|27] [smiled|29] at the [trick|28] that she [had seen through|31].",
