 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np9-01",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Epithet",
   "quote": "regia Iuno",
   "trans": "queenly Juno",
   "line": "114",
   "body": "regia stresses Juno's rank as queen of the gods, and the phrase matches Dido's own status as a queen.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-02",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "mecum erit iste labor",
   "trans": "that task will be mine",
   "line": "115",
   "body": "mecum comes first: Juno takes charge of the plan herself, confident and in control.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-03",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "paucis adverte docebo",
   "trans": "listen, I will explain in a few words",
   "line": "116",
   "body": "Juno speaks like a general giving orders. Her confident, businesslike tone contrasts with the passion of the mortals she is planning for.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-04",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "unaque miserrima Dido",
   "trans": "and with him most wretched Dido",
   "line": "117",
   "body": "The superlative miserrima is Virgil's own comment, slipped into Juno's speech: Dido is doomed, and the narrator pities her.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-05",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Imagery of light",
   "quote": "primos crastinus ortus extulerit Titan radiisque retexerit orbem",
   "trans": "when tomorrow's sun has raised its first rising and uncovered the world with its rays",
   "line": "118–119",
   "body": "The grand description of sunrise gives the plan an epic setting. The idea of the world being \"uncovered\" contrasts with the darkness that will hide the lovers.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-06",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Storm imagery",
   "quote": "nigrantem commixta grandine nimbum",
   "trans": "a black cloud mixed with hail",
   "line": "120",
   "body": "The storm Juno promises is dark and violent, an ominous backdrop for a \"wedding\".",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-07",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Vivid detail",
   "quote": "dum trepidant alae saltusque indagine cingunt",
   "trans": "while the riders bustle and surround the glades with nets",
   "line": "121",
   "body": "Juno pictures the hunt in motion, so the storm will strike when everyone is busy and scattered.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-08",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Hunting",
   "quote": "saltusque indagine cingunt",
   "trans": "surround the glades with nets",
   "line": "121",
   "body": "The hunters will be trapping animals, but Dido herself is the one being trapped, which recalls the simile of the wounded deer.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-09",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Onomatopoeia",
   "quote": "tonitru caelum omne ciebo",
   "trans": "I will shake the whole sky with thunder",
   "line": "122",
   "body": "The hard c and t sounds suggest the crash of the thunder Juno will send.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-10",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Darkness",
   "quote": "nocte tegentur opaca",
   "trans": "will be covered by dark night",
   "line": "123",
   "body": "The companions will be hidden by darkness, an image of secrecy that suits the shameful nature of what follows.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-11",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Word order",
   "quote": "speluncam Dido dux et Troianus eandem",
   "trans": "Dido and the Trojan leader to the same cave",
   "line": "124",
   "body": "speluncam ... eandem frames the two lovers, so the word order traps them in the cave together.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-12",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Formal language",
   "quote": "conubio iungam stabili propriamque dicabo",
   "trans": "I will join them in lasting marriage and declare her his own",
   "line": "126",
   "body": "Juno uses the formal words of a Roman wedding. The promise of a \"lasting\" marriage is ironic, as it will last only a few months.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-13",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "hic hymenaeus erit",
   "trans": "this will be their wedding",
   "line": "127",
   "body": "A short, final phrase: Juno announces the \"wedding\" bluntly, which makes it sound more like a trap than a marriage.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-14",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Litotes",
   "quote": "non adversata petenti",
   "trans": "not opposing her request",
   "line": "127",
   "body": "Venus agrees by not refusing, which shows how reluctant and calculating her consent is.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-15",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "dolis risit Cytherea repertis",
   "trans": "Venus smiled at the tricks she had seen through",
   "line": "128",
   "body": "Venus smiles because she understands Juno's scheme and knows it will fail. The goddesses' amusement contrasts with the tragedy waiting for Dido.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-16",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Titan",
   "quote": "Titan",
   "trans": "the Sun",
   "line": "119",
   "body": "The sun, here called Titan because the sun god Helios (or Sol) was the son of the Titan Hyperion.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np9-17",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Cytherea",
   "quote": "Cytherea",
   "trans": "Venus",
   "line": "128",
   "body": "A title of Venus, from the island of Cythera off the south of the Peloponnese, near which she was said to have risen from the sea.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 9,
 "questions": [
  "Juno's plan is set out in great detail (117–127). What does the level of detail tell us about her as a planner, and about how little Dido and Aeneas control their own lives?",
  "Is the \"marriage\" in the cave a real marriage? Look closely at Juno's words in 126–127 and compare them with what you know happens later.",
  "Why does Venus smile at the end of the section, and what does her smile suggest about the outcome?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "The cave and the storm",
   "body": "<p>The plan Juno sets out here is carried out in lines 129–172, which are not part of the set text. Dido and Aeneas ride out to the hunt in great splendour; the storm breaks; everyone runs for shelter; and the two of them find themselves alone in the same cave. Earth and Juno give the signal, lightning flashes like wedding torches, and nymphs cry out on the mountain tops.</p><p>Virgil comments that this day was the first cause of death and of evils. Dido calls it a marriage, but Aeneas never does, and the difference between them on this point lies at the heart of their later quarrel.</p>"
  }
 ]
}